INSERT INTO `knowledge_documents` (`id`, `knowledge_base_id`, `title`, `content`, `file_path`, `file_type`, `tags`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `authors`, `journal`, `pub_year`, `doi`, `pmid`, `pmcid`, `source_url`, `keywords`) VALUES (1,3,'算法与数据结构','算法是解决问题的步骤序列。数据结构是组织和存储数据的方式。常见的数据结构包括数组、链表、栈、队列、树和图。排序算法如快速排序、归并排序的时间复杂度为O(nlogn)。',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-03-02 12:26:50','2026-03-02 12:26:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `knowledge_documents` (`id`, `knowledge_base_id`, `title`, `content`, `file_path`, `file_type`, `tags`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `authors`, `journal`, `pub_year`, `doi`, `pmid`, `pmcid`, `source_url`, `keywords`) VALUES (2,12,'lq-paper1','Copyedited by: DS\nJournal of\n Plant EcologyJOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | https://doi.org/10.1093/jpe/rtae027 1\n© The Author(s) 2024. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the Institute of Botany, Chinese Academy of Sciences and the Botanical Society of China.\nThis is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted reuse, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.Research Article\nThe responses of photosynthetic rate of dominant species to \nextreme drought in Inner Mongolia grasslands\nYalong  Shi1,2,†,, Yuanbo  Cao3,†, Honghui  Wu1,2,*, Chong  Xu1,2, Qiang  Yu4, Xiaoan  Zuo5, \nXingguo  Han6, Melinda D.  Smith7,, Alan K.  Knapp7, Chengjie  Wang8 and Guodong  Han8\n1State Key Laboratory of Efficient Utilization of Arid and Semi-arid Arable Land in Northern China, Institute of Agricultural Resources \nand Regional Planning, Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences, Beijing 100081, China, 2Key Laboratory of Arable Land Quality \nMonitoring and Evaluation, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Institute of Agricultural Resources and Regional Planning, \nChinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences, Beijing 100081, China, 3China Rida Investment and Development Group Corp Ltd, Beijing \n100040, China, 4School of Grassland Science, Beijing Forestry University, Beijing 100083, China, 5Urat Desert-grassland Research \nStation, Northwest Institute of Eco-Environment and Resources, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Lanzhou 730000, China, 6State Key \nLaboratory of Vegetation and Environmental Change, Institute of Botany, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100093, China, \n7Department of Biology and Graduate Degree Program in Ecology, Colorado State University, Fort Collins, CO 80523, USA, 8College of \nGrassland, Resource and Environmental, Inner Mongolia Agricultural University, Hohhot 010011, China\n*Corresponding author. E-mail: wuhonghui@caas.cn\n†These authors contributed equally to this work.\nHandling Editor: Nianpeng  He\nReceived: 9 March 2024, First Decision: 19 March 2024, Accepted: 2 April 2024, Online Publication: 9 April 2024\nAbstract \nEstimating the effects of extreme drought on the photosynthetic rates (Pn) of dominant plant species is crucial \nfor understanding the mechanisms driving the impacts of extreme drought on ecosystem functioning. Extreme drought may result from either reduced rainfall amount or decreased rainfall frequency, and the impacts of different patterns of extreme drought may vary greatly. In addition, different grasslands likely appear various sensitivity to different extreme drought patterns. However, there have been no reports on the effects of different extreme drought patterns on dominant species Pn in different grassland types. Here, we conducted multi-year extreme drought simulation experiments (reducing each rainfall event by 66% during the growing season, CHR vs. completely excluding rainfall during a shorter portion of the growing season, INT) in two different \ngrasslands (desert grassland vs. typical grassland) from 2014. The Pn of two dominant species in each grassland was measured in July and August 2017. Both CHR and INT significantly decreased dominant species Pn, with INT causing more negative impacts on Pn regardless of grassland types. The response ratios of Pn in desert grassland were generally higher than that of typical grassland, especially for Leymus chinensis in CHR. These results demonstrated that decreased rainfall frequency had a more negative effect on Pn compared with reduced rainfall amount, with grassland types changing the magnitude, but not the direction, of the effects of extreme drought patterns. These findings highlight the importance of considering extreme drought patterns and grassland types in ecosystem management in the face of future extreme droughts.\nKeywords: climate extremes, desert grassland, typical grassland, extreme drought pattern, seasonal droughtDownloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS2 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027内蒙古草原优势物种光合速率对极端干旱的响应\n摘要：评估极端干旱对优势植物物种光合速率 (Pn)的影响对于我们理解极端干旱影响生态系统功能的机\n制至关重要。极端干旱可能由降雨量减少或降雨频率减少造成。这两种不同模式的极端干旱可能造成\n不同的影响。此外，不同草原类型可能对这两种极端干旱模式表现出不同的敏感性。然而，目前尚不清\n楚这两种极端干旱模式对草原优势植物物种 Pn的影响有何差异，以及在不同草原类型这两种不同模式  \n的极端干旱的效应是否改变。为此，我们于 2014年在内蒙古草原两个不同类型的草原 (荒漠草原 vs.典\n型草原)站点各进行了一项多年的极端干旱(CHR：将生长季期间每次降雨事件的降雨量减少66% vs. \nINT：完全排除生长季内一段较短时间的降水 )模拟实验。于 2017年的 7月和8月，我们在这两个草原站\n点各测量了两种优势物种的 Pn。结果发现， CHR和INT均显著降低了优势物种的 Pn。无论草原类型如\n何，INT对Pn造成的负面影响都更大。在荒漠草原，极端干旱对 Pn的负面影响普遍大于典型草原，尤其\n是对于 CHR处理下的羊草 (Leymus  chinensis )。这些结果表明，与降雨量减少相比，降雨频率减少所引起\n的极端干旱对Pn 的负面影响更大。草地类型可能会改变极端干旱模式效应的程度，但不会改变其方向。\n这些发现强调了应对未来极端干旱的草原生态系统管理应考虑极端干旱模式和草地类型的影响。\n关键词：极端气候，荒漠草原，典型草原，极端干旱模式，季节性干旱\nINTRODUCTION\nExtreme droughts are defined as precipitation events \nthat fall below the 5th percentile of the historical range of variability (Smith 2011). They have the potential to cause widespread importance on ecosystem functions and processes ( Hopkins and Del Prado 2007 ; Ma et al. \n2023; Smith 2011) through affecting plants growth \nand production (Dumont et al. 2015; Knapp et al.  \n2015; Wei et al. 2022). Extreme drought may result \nfrom either reduction in rainfall amount or decrease in rainfall frequency, and the impacts of different patterns of extreme drought on ecosystems may vary greatly (Goke and Martin 2023). Clarifying the differences in \nthe impacts of different extreme drought patterns is important for ecosystem management in the face of \nfuture extreme droughts.\nThe photosynthesis is the basis for plant growth and \nproduction (Garcia et al. 2023). The different extreme \ndrought patterns might cause different negative effects \non plant photosynthetic rates (Pn) ( Li et al. 2024). \nHowever, most studies have primarily focused on the \ndrought caused by reduced rainfall amount, while the effects of drought resulted from a decrease in rainfall frequency have been relatively neglected ( Luo et al. \n2023; Sun et al. 2023). With the progression of climate \nchange, both the frequency and magnitude of extreme drought have markedly increased ( Ma et al. 2023; Wei \net al. 2022). Extreme drought events may persist for several weeks or even months, leading to a pronounced alteration in frequency ( Ma et al. 2023), underscoring \nthe significance of studying rainfall frequency. Currently, research on the ecological responses to \nextreme drought patterns has predominantly centered on biomass or primary productivity ( Carroll et al.  \n2021), with less emphasis on comparing the effects of \nextreme drought characterized by a decrease in rainfall \nfrequency or reduction in rainfall amount on Pn.\nGrassland ecosystems inherently exhibit \ncomplexity due to the presence of diverse biotic components such as species assemblages, and abiotic components including soil, precipitation and \ntemperature, along with their associated processes \n(Jaman et al. 2022; Lüscher et al. 2022; Stuart-\nHaëntjens et al. 2018). Consequently, the impacts of \nextreme drought can vary significantly across distinct \ngrassland ecosystems. The previous studies suggest \ntwo distinct hypotheses regarding the response of different ecosystems to extreme drought ( Hoover et al. \n2021; Ilyas et al. 2021). One hypothesis highlighted \nthat wetter locations are more sensitive than drier ones, as plants in arid regions are accustomed to enduring water stress and have adapted to drought conditions (Byrne et al. 2013; Grime 1997\n; Tielbörger \net al. 2014). The other claimed that drier locations are more sensitive than wetter ones, since in moist \nenvironments, water is not the primary limiting \nfactor as it is in arid ecosystems ( Huxman et al. 2004; \nKnapp et al. 2015; Stuart-Haëntjens et al. 2018). The \ndebates stimulated comparative studies of the effects \nof extreme drought in different grasslands in order \nto find general rules (Knapp et al. 2015; Yahdjian \net al. 2021). The effects of extreme drought on photosynthetic rate may be a potential mechanism Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS3 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027underlying the differential responses of different \ngrasslands to extreme drought ( Jia et al. 2023; Knapp \net al. 2020; Yang et al. 2023). However, knowledge \nabout how photosynthetic rates respond to extreme drought patterns in different grassland ecosystems remains elusive.\nThe mechanisms driving drought effects on Pn have \nbeen extensively investigated ( Li et al. 2023; Vitale \net al. 2009). Drought can lead to the closure of leaf stomata and a decrease in transpiration ( Li et al. 2023; \nVitale et al. 2009), disrupting the water balance within \nplants (Flexas et al. 2006) and then affecting the Pn. \nStomatal closure can reduce stomatal conductance and vapor pressure deficit, potentially hindering the assimilation of carbon dioxide ( Tor-ngern et al. 2021). \nAdditionally, the carbon dioxide produced during plant dissimilation may not be efficiently released, further impairing the photosynthetic process ( Ort \nand Baker 2002). The impact of extreme drought on photosynthesis may exhibit nonlinear behavior due to physiological trade-offs, introducing uncertain effects on the photosynthetic process ( Flexas et al. \n2006; Vitale et al. 2009). Therefore, establishing the \nrelationship between Pn and photosynthetic traits can provide a comprehensive assessment of Pn response to extreme drought.\nThe Inner Mongolia grassland is experiencing \nan escalation in both the frequency and intensity of extreme drought events ( Meehl et al. 2007) which \nthreatens local ecosystem functions and services. Thus, there is an urgent need to investigate the effects of extreme drought on these grassland ecosystems. In this study, we assess the responses of the photosynthesis rates (Pn) of dominant plant species to chronic drought (CHR, reducing each rainfall event by 66% during the growing season) and intense drought (INT, completely excluding rainfall during a shorter portion of the growing season) in two distinct grasslands (desert grassland vs. typical grassland) in the Inner Mongolia grassland. We addressed the following three questions: (i) how extreme droughts affect dominant species Pn; (ii) whether extreme drought patterns influence the responses of Pn; (iii) whether Pn appeared varied sensitivity to extreme drought patterns in desert grassland and typical grassland.\nMATERIALS AND METHODS\nExperimental sites\nThis study was conducted at two distinct sites: one located in Sheila MuRen (111°53ʹ E, 41°47 ʹ N), characterized by a desert grassland environment, and the other in Xilingol (116°42ʹ E, 43°38ʹ N), representing a typical grassland habitat. Sheila MuRen experiences a mean annual precipitation of \n236 mm, with a mean temperature of 3.4°C and an \nannual potential evaporation rate of 2227  mm. The \nsoil is classified as chestnut soil, containing 2%–4% soil organic matter, 0.1%–0.15% soil total nitrogen, 0.08%–0.15% soil total phosphorus and 2%–2.5% soil total potassium, with a pH range of 8–8.5. Dominant species in this area include Stipa krylovii , \nCaragana stenophylla, Leymus chinensis, Artemisia frigida, \nCaragana microphylla and Cleistogenes squarrosa. In \ncontrast, Xilingol receives a mean annual precipitation \nof 346  mm, with a mean temperature of 2.3°C and an \nannual potential evaporation rate of 1862  mm. The \nsoil type is also chestnut soil, containing 1.6%–3.7% soil organic matter, 0.12%–0.18% soil total nitrogen, 0.1%–0.17% soil total phosphorus and 1.8%–2.6% soil total potassium, with a pH range of 7.8–8.2. Dominant species in Xilingol include L. chinensis, Stipa \ngrandis, Achnatherum sibiricum , Agropyron cristatum,  \nC. microphylla and Salsola collina.\nExperimental design\nThe experiments were set up in 2014, and the treatments have been conducted since 2015. There were three treatments in each site, i.e. control (CON), chronic drought (CHR) and intense drought (INT) with six replicates. CHR reduced each rainfall event by 66% during the growing season (from May to August), while INT completely excluded rainfall from 1 June to 31 July (Luo et al. 2022\n). The size of the shelters is 6.2 m  ×  6.2  \nm, which is fully covered the size of the experimental \nplots (6 m  ×  6 m) ( Griffin-Nolan et al. 2019; Yahdjian \nand Sala 2002 ). The roof (consist of clear acrylic with \nthe characteristic of high light transmission, low yellowness index and UV transparent) of each modular unit will be slanted at 15° toward the edge of the plot, and the lower edge will be oriented to the prevailing wind direction to minimize blow-in ( Griffin-Nolan et al. \n2019). In order to reduce the marginal effect, we only \nuse the middle area (4 m  ×  4 m) as the sampling area. \nTo prevent water exchange between individual plots, we implemented hydrological isolation by installing waterproof plastic boards along the boundaries of each plot to a depth of 1 m.\nData measurements\nWe measured monthly precipitation and Pn in 2017. Precipitation was recorded by the weather station near Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS4 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027each experiment site. In Sheila MuRen, the precipitation \nfor the CHR and INT treatments was 97.26 and \n85.30  mm, respectively ( Fig. 1a). The reductions in \nprecipitation for the CHR and INT treatments were \n134.64 and 146.60  mm, respectively . In Xilingol, the \nprecipitation for the CHR and INT treatments were \n139.90 and 169.60  mm, respectively ( Fig. 1b). The \nreductions in precipitation of CHR and INT were 115.70 \nand 86.00  mm, respectively . We measured Pn at the \nbeginning and end of July and August, with the mean \nvalue of twice measurements each month representing the level of Pn in that month. The two most dominant species were selected in each site, i.e. S. krylovii and \nL. chinensis in Sheila MuRen and S. grandis and L. chinensis  \nin Xilingol. The selected species at different sites belonged \nto the same species or genus to minimize the impact of species-specific responses on the assessment of extreme drought effects. For each species, four healthy and representative plant individuals were selected randomly in each plot, and each plant was measured three times with three different leaves between 11:00 and 13:00, using a Portable Photosynthesis System (Li-6400, Lincoln, NE, USA) with red and blue artificial light source. Measuring during the midday period allowed us to focus on maximum photosynthetic activity and avoid potential confounding factors associated with morning or afternoon extremes. All indices were measured during periods characterized by the absence of precipitation events on the preceding day and clear sky conditions on the day of measurement. The target plants were labeled with flags to ensure that the same plants were measured each time. The measurements were performed with the photosynthetic photon flux density of 1500 µmol m\n−2 s−1, the CO2 concentration \nof 400 µmol mol−1 and the leaf temperature of 30°C. \nThe photosynthetic photon flux density closely approximated the light intensity experienced by plants in full sunlight on a clear day which ensured that photosynthesis is operating at its maximum rate. At a leaf temperature of 30°C, most plant species exhibited optimal photosynthetic activity, which ensured that photosynthesis is not limited by temperature. The infrared gas analyzer simultaneously recorded the following four photosynthetic traits: transpiration rate (Tr, mmol H\n2O m−2 s−1), vapor pressure deficit ( Vpdl, Pa), \nintercellular CO2 concentration (Ci, µmol CO2 mol−1) \nand stomatal conductance (Gs, mol H2O m−2 s−1).\nData analysis\nThe response ratios (RR) of Pn to extreme drought was defined as \nRR=Pn(CON -Treatments) /Pn (CON ),  \ntreatments represent CHR or INT. The sensitivity of Pn represents the change of photosynthetic rate caused by per unit of precipitation ( P) variation, i.e. \nSensitivity =Pn(CON -Treatments) /P(CON -Treatments ). The \nformula WUE=Pn/Tr  is used to calculate the water \nuse efficiency. Repeated measures analysis of variance were used to analyze the difference of Pn, the RR and the sensitivity among treatments, between months and sites. Significant differences were estimated at the level of 1% ( P\n < 0.01) using T ukey’s test. Linear \nregression equations or quadratic equations were used to determine the relationship between Pn and photosynthetic traits (Tr, V\npdl, Ci, Gs and WUE). All \nthe statistical analyses were conducted using SPSS software (SPSS v.19; IBM, Chicago IL, USA).\nFigure 1:  The monthly precipitation in Sheila MuRen (a) and Xilingol (b) in 2017. Abbreviations: CON = control, CHR = \nchronic drought (reducing each rainfall event by 66% from May to August), INT = intense drought (completely excluding \nrainfall from 1 June to 31 July).Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS5 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027RESULTS\nEffects of extreme drought on plant \nphotosynthetic rates\nBoth chronic drought (CHR) and intense drought \n(INT) significantly decreased photosynthetic rates (Pn) across the species and months in both the desert and typical grasslands ( Fig. 2). The Pn under INT were \ngenerally lower than those under CHR (Fig. 2). On average, Pn was decreased by 35.43% and 45.21% in CHR and INT, respectively. The Pn in August was generally lower than those in July (all P\n < 0.01).\nThe RR of Pn in INT consistently exceeded that \nin CHR (Fig. 3). The RR of Pn in Sheila MuRen was higher than that in Xilingol (P  < 0.01). On average, \nthe RR of Pn in Sheila MuRen and Xilingol was \n0.50 and 0.36, respectively. The RR in August was generally higher than that in July (all P\n < 0.01, except \nfor L. chinensis in Sheila MuRen). The differences in \nPn sensitivity between sites and months exhibited opposing patterns compared with the differences in RR (Fig. 4), while the extreme drought patterns had similar effects on Pn and RR across species.\nThe effect of extreme drought on the \nrelationships between photosynthetic rates and \nphotosynthesis traits\nIn each treatment of two sites, linear correlations \noccurred between the Pn and transpiration rate (Tr), \nFigure 2:  The effects of extreme drought on photosynthetic rates (Pn) in Sheila MuRen (a) and Xilingol (b). Different capital \nletters indicate significant differences among treatments ( P < 0.01). Abbreviations: CON = control, CHR = chronic drought \n(reducing each rainfall event by 66% from May to August), INT = intense drought (completely excluding rainfall from 1 \nJune to 31 July). Error bars represent mean  ±  SE.\nFigure 3:  The response ratios (RR) of photosynthetic rates (Pn) to extreme drought in Sheila MuRen  (a) and Xilingol (b). \nDifferent capital letters indicate significant differences among treatments ( P < 0.01). Abbreviations: CHR = chronic drought \n(reducing each rainfall event by 66% from May to August), INT = intense drought (completely excluding rainfall from 1 \nJune to 31 July). Error bars represent mean  ±  SE.Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS\n6 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027between Pn and vapor pressure deficit ( Vpdl, except \nthe INT-Leymus in Sheila MuRen), between Pn and \nintercellular CO2 concentration (Ci, except the CHR-\nStipa in Sheila MuRen and Xilingol), between the Pn \nand stomatal conductance ( Gs), between Pn and \nwater use efficiency (WUE, except the CHR- Stipa in \nSheila MuRen, the CHR-Stipa, CHR-Leymus and INT-\nLeymus in Xilingol) (Table 1). In different treatments, \nthe slopes of the linear equation were different and mostly showed the pattern: INT > CHR > CON (Table  1).\nDISCUSSION\nThere is increasing evidence that the frequency and severity of extreme drought have already increased in many regions ( Spinoni et al. 2018). It is not just the \ndecrease in rainfall amount, the frequency of rainfall events has also seen a severe reduction. In the grasslands of Inner Mongolia, both the number and duration of extreme drought events have increased significantly, with even previously humid areas transitioning to dry conditions (An 2016; Di et al. \n2022). While previous studies have primarily focused on the effects of extreme drought resulted from a reduction in the amount of rainfall event, there has been limited research on the impacts of decreased rainfall frequency ( Huang et al. 2023; Zhang et al. \n2016). In this study, we conducted two extreme drought treatments (reducing each rainfall event by 66% from May to August, CHR vs. completely \nexcluding rainfall from 1 June to 31 July, INT) and measured the responses of plant photosynthetic rates (Pn). We found that INT consistently had a greater impact on Pn compared with CHR, despite the INT treatment experiencing a weaker reduction in rainfall (Fig. 1). This suggests that a decrease in rainfall frequency may improve extreme drought effects on Pn. The previous study showed that INT caused more negative impacts on plant community productivity compared with CHR in Konza Prairie Biological Station (Carroll et al. 2021). Our study providing \nfurther clues to plant physiological responses to explain the effects of extreme drought patterns. Overall, these results highlight the importance of considering extreme drought patterns in ecosystem management and conservation efforts.\nPrevious studies have indicated that the effects \nof extreme drought on Pn vary across ecosystems and are often nonlinear. For instance, Gao et al. \n(2019) observed that the Pn of Festuca arundinacea  \nremained unchanged when precipitation decreased by 50% but decreased significantly by 51.9% when precipitation decreased by 70% in a relatively mesic ecosystem. In contrast, another study showed a different pattern in a relatively arid ecosystem, where the Pn of Lolium multiflorum decreased by \n30.7% with a 50% reduction in precipitation, and by 45.0% with a 70% reduction in precipitation (Li et al. 2018). Thus, while low-intensity drought \nmay elicit varying Pn responses in mesic and arid ecosystems, extreme drought is likely to induce similar responses in Pn across different ecosystems. This finding corresponded well with previous studies \nthat deserts, grasslands and forests exhibit the same rate of biomass reduction when water is extremely \nFigure 4:  Effect of extreme drought on the sensitivity of photosynthetic rates (Pn) in Sheila MuRen (a) and Xilingol  \n(b). Different capital letters indicate significant differences among treatments ( P < 0.01). Abbreviations: CHR = chronic \ndrought (reducing each rainfall event by 66% from May to August), INT = intense drought (completely excluding rainfall \nfrom 1 June to 31 July). Error bars represent mean  ±  SE.Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS\n7 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027Table 1:  The effects of extreme drought on the relationships between photosynthetic rates (Pn) and photosynthesis traits\nSitesPhysiological \nfactors Tr (N = 91) Vpdl (N = 90) Ci (N = 85) Gs (N = 67) WUE (N  = 80)\nSheila \nMuRenCON-Stipa y = 2.64 x + 1.4