y = 1.94 x + 2.28\nR2 = 0.54, P = 0.00y = 1.70 x − 1.00\nR2 = 0.37, P = 0.00- y = 86.76 x + 2.22\nR2 = 0.49, P = 0.00y = −0.74 x2 + 5.92 x − 4.23\nR2 = 0.31, P = 0.09\nINT-Leymus y = 3.68 x + 1.66\nR2 = 0.57, P = 0.00y = 1.51 x + 1.23\nR2 = 0.32, P = 0.00y = −0.015 x + 7.30\nR2 = 0.51, P = 0.00y = 110.97 x + 0.13\nR2 = 0.46, P = 0.00y = −0.29 x2 + 3.80 x − 3.52\nR2 = 0.49, P = 0.04\nNote: ‘-’ represents no significant regression relationships. Abbreviations: Ci = intercellular CO2 concentration, Gs = stomatal conductance, Tr = transpiration rate, Vpdl = \nvapor pressure deficit.Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS\n8 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027limiting (Huxman et al. 2004; Knapp et al. 2015; Wang et \nal. 2022). Our results provide physiologic information \nfor above-ground  net primary productivity (ANPP)  \nevaluation and thus are very helpful to explore the \nmechanisms underlying the extreme drought impacts on ecosystem function and processes.\nThe impacts of CHR on Pn accumulated from \nJuly to August ( Fig. 3). This could be caused by the \naccumulated negative effects of extreme drought on soil moisture (Hoover et al. 2021). This aligns with \nthe results of a potted experiment, where after 6 days of drought resulting in a 50% reduction in soil water content, there was no change in the Pn of Coronilla \nvaria (Yang et al. 2013). However, by the 12th day, \nPn had decreased by 24.1%, and by the 18th day, it had declined by 42.5% ( Yang et al. 2013). For the INT \ntreatment, the decline in Pn continued to exacerbate in August, indicating the delayed effects of extreme droughts. It implies that even as climatic conditions return to normal, there likely remains a prolonged water deficit (Hoover et al. 2021), leading to a \ncontinual decline in photosynthetic rates following the end of the drought period.\nThe impact of extreme drought on Pn varied \nsignificantly among different plant species and across various sites (Flexas et al. 2022). Our findings revealed \nthat, compared with the CON, extreme drought led to a substantial decrease in Pn levels: S. krylovii by \n46.67% and L. chinensis by 50.01% in Sheila MuRen \nand S. grandis by 37.62% and L. chinensis by 32.72% \nin Xilingol. These diverse responses observed among \nspecies can be attributed to complex physiological and ecological processes, including trade-offs between WUE and growth rate, specific plant genotypes and long-term environmental adaptation (Smith et al. 2009; Xu et al. 2021). Furthermore, the \neffects of extreme drought on Pn varied between the two study sites. Notably, the impact on L. chinensis in \nSheila MuRen was more pronounced compared with that in Xilingol. While mean annual precipitation \nwas considered the primary factor influencing the differential responses among ecosystems, the characteristics of dominant species and plant functional diversity also played significant roles (Luo et al. 2021; Miao et al. 2022; Wang et al. 2023). \nHence, conducting experiments across multiple sites is essential to comprehensively assess both biotic and abiotic determinants of ecosystem responses to extreme drought.\nThere are two primary methods for investigating \nthe relative response of ecosystems to climate change or human disturbance. One method is sensitivity, which measures the change caused by each unit of precipitation, while the other is the RR, which compares the relative change with a control (Huxman et al. 2004; Knapp et al. 2015). \nOur results indicated that the sensitivity of Pn in Sheila MuRen\n (mean annual precipitation 236  mm)  \nwas generally lower than that in Xilingol (mean \nannual precipitation 346  mm), suggesting that Pn  \nsensitivity increases with precipitation. In contrast, studies conducted in North American grasslands showed that the sensitivity of ANPP decreased with increasing precipitation (Huxman et al. 2004). \nThis difference may be attributed to the different strategies employed by plants to cope with drought stress. In Inner Mongolia, ecosystems are much drier than most grasslands in North America, and plants are more constrained by trade-offs with traits for stress tolerance (Liu and Li 2020; Smith et al.  \n2009). However, an opposite pattern was found for the RR of Pn, suggesting that in arid sites, Pn was more severely affected by extreme droughts compared with relatively wet sites when compared with a control. Thus, RR and sensitivity of Pn showed completely opposite patterns in response to extreme drought in our study sites, indicating that the sensitivity of ecosystem functions may differ (even show opposite trends) depending on the method used.\nPlants typically employ protective mechanisms \nto alleviate damage caused by extreme drought stress, allowing them to withstand harsh conditions (Ilyas et al. 2021; Wahab et al. 2022). Adjustments \nin transpiration rate, vapor pressure deficit, intercellular CO\n2 concentration and stomatal \nconductance represent specific responses to extreme drought, providing effective strategies for combating stress (Ge et al. 2016; Grossiord et  al.  \n2020\n). The relationships between a  plant\'s  Pn  \nand its photosynthetic traits, both of which are influenced by water availability, may exhibit some variability (Gonzalez-Paleo and Ravetta 2018 ; \nMueller et al. 2022). In our study, we observed that \nthe two extreme drought patterns generally did not alter the fundamental relationship between photosynthetic traits and Pn. However, they did affect the slopes of the linear equations describing this relationship. Specifically, the slopes of the linear functions for the INT and CHR treatments were greater than that of the CON treatment, indicating that extreme drought intensified the response of Pn to photosynthetic trait factors. A closer relationship between photosynthetic traits and Pn may represent Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/jpe/article/17/3/rtae027/7642563 by guest on 21 January 2026\n\nCopyedited by: DS\n9 JOURNAL OF PLANT ECOLOGY | 2024, 17:rtae027a double-edged sword: while it may facilitate faster \nrecovery of plant Pn once extreme drought is alleviated, prolonged exposure to extreme drought conditions may lead to greater damage or even collapse of Pn.\nCONCLUSIONS\nAim to clarify the impact of extreme drought patterns on the photosynthetic rates (Pn) of dominant plant species in different grassland environments, we conducted experiments involving two extreme drought patterns (chronic drought: CHR, reducing rainfall by 66% from May to August; intense drought: INT, reducing rainfall by 100% from June to July) in both desert grassland and typical grassland environments. We found that both CHR and INT significantly decreased the Pn of dominant species in July and August in both types of grasslands, with Pn decreasing by an average of 35.43% and 45.21% in CHR and INT, respectively. Moreover, we observed that the RR of Pn in desert grassland was generally higher than that of typical grassland, indicating a greater sensitivity to extreme drought in desert grasslands. These findings underscore the importance of considering different drought patterns and grassland types in ecosystem management and conservation efforts. By understanding the mechanisms driving the response of dominant plant species to extreme drought, we can better inform strategies to mitigate the impacts of drought on ecosystem functions and processes in arid and semi-arid grasslands.\nFunding\nThis work was supported by National Key Research and \nDevelopment Program of China  (2019YFE0117000) \nand National Natural Science Foundation of China \n(32171651, 31971533).\nAcknowledgements\nWe thank Dr Yunlong Zhang and Minghui Jing for their help during field sampling and Prof. Kebiao Mao for providing his meteorological data during thesis writing.Confict of interest statement. The authors declare that \nthey have no 